/dev/null; ls .entire/runners; head -c 700 .entire/runners/trail-review.json","description":"Read review/merge sections and a runner config","timeout":120000}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_016kvBKaAhL94Uq8jY1tG5WJ":{"cwd":"/Volumes/PJ/peresjav/hackathons/ehl-final-minuch-2026"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfuKeNt5wpG724uko8QN1","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"785c0818-aab1-488a-bf5f-e4aeeef14b39","timestamp":"2026-10-10T22:10:52.756Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"53e0d48f-0e86-4176-8390-70cad3fe3f46","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Volumes/PJ/peresjav/hackathons/ehl-final-minuch-2026","sessionId":"7fd52f13-5d0c-4e7f-8ec6-8a947b2499a1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"federated-petting-ripple"}
{"parentUuid":"785c0818-aab1-488a-bf5f-e4aeeef14b39","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e297afe6-99ef-4e4d-b227-a8147ae9952e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016kvBKaAhL94Uq8jY1tG5WJ","type":"tool_result","content":"2. Build the Change 3. Review the Trail 4. Approve and Merge One-Time Runner Setup For Repo Owners Add the Default Runners Configure Merge Gates Shell Shell Key Terms First, a few definitions, so you can speak our language. Checkpoint: a checkpoint is basically an autosave, a point at which your work is saved without committing. Finding: an issue with your code that prevents a trail from merging. Gate: a merge requirement. Runner: evaluation metrics that inform the gates. Monitor: a judgment “scorecardˮ monitoring verification status against the runners. Ours run Risk, Confidence, Security, and Slop. If You Donʼt Already Have Entire Setup The steps below assume Entire is installed on your machine and your repository is connected to entire.io with trails turned on. If your team has already done this, start at step 1. 1. Install the CLI brew tap entireio/tap brew trust entireio/tap brew install --cask entire On Linux, run curl -fsSL https://entire.io/install.sh | bash . On Windows, use Scoop: scoop bucket add entire https://github.com/entireio/scoop-bucket.git , then scoop install entire/entire. 2. Confirm the Install entire version Shell Shell 3. Sign In to Entire entire login 4. Enable Entire in Your Repository This installs the Git and agent hooks and creates the .entire/ settings your team shares: entire enable Pass --agent to skip the prompt, for example entire enable --agent claude-code. 5. Connect the Repository to entire.io Open repository setup, select Configure GitHub App, and give Entire access to the repository. A repository owned by a GitHub organization needs an organization admin to install or update the app. 6. Turn On Trails Open the repository in entire.io, select Settings , then turn on: - Runners - Trails under Workflow - Auto build on push. This requires admin permission on the repository. Once the repository appears in entire.io with trails turned on, continue with the steps below. If You Do Already Have Entire Setup 0. Turn on Trails if you havenʼt already (see above) 1. Create a Trail In entire.io 1. Open a repository and select Trails. 2. Select New Trail. Shell Shell Shell 3. Enter a title and, if you like, a description. A good description covers the problem, intended outcome, and any important constraints or acceptance criteria. 4. Choose the status, type, assignees, and priority. 5. Leave the Trail branchless, enter a new branch name, or link an existing branch. 6. Select Create Trail. In the CLI 1. Open a terminal in your Git repository. 2. Run the interactive creation flow: entire trail create 3. To skip the form, create a Trail for the current branch by passing a title: entire trail create --title \"Add account recovery\" Add --checkout to switch to a new branch after Entire creates it from the title. 2. Build the Change 1. Work on the Trailʼs branch with your coding agent. Entire adds the resulting checkpoints and agent sessions to the Trail. 2. Commit your changes when theyʼre ready for evaluation: a. Tell your agent to add and commit b. OR git add . git commit -m \"Add account recovery\" 3. Push the branch: a. ask the agent to push the trail. The agent recognizes the trail connected to the branch with the help of the CLI and it will take care of committing and pushing. b. OR Shell git push 3. Review the Trail 1. Read the description and confirm that the implementation matches the intended outcome. 2. Check the Confidence , Risk , Security , and Drift monitors for anything that needs attention. 3. Select View changes and review the diff. 4. If there are code review findings, open each finding and decide whether to fix, resolve, or dismiss it. 5. Use Sessions and the timeline when you need the agent context behind a decision. After another push, findings tied to older code may become stale. Current findings remain open until they are resolved or dismissed. Since your coding agent interfaces with Entire through the CLI, you can also complete the loop from your agent, for example by telling the agent to review open findings on the trail and address them. 4. Approve and Merge Gate What it Evaluates Options Up to date Whether the Trail branch is current with its base Allow a clean merge while behind Human review Approvals submitted on the Trail Minimum approvals, whether the author counts, and whether pushes reset approvals Agent review Unresolved runner findings on the latest push Minimum severity that counts as unresolved Checks CI checks reported through GitHub Use GitHub-required checks or provide specific check names 1. Review the gates section for anything blocking the merge. Gates show which conditions are satisfied, pending, or blocking the merge. A repository can require an up-to-date branch, human approvals, an agent review without unresolved findings, and passing checks. You can also choose who may bypass a blocking gate. Every bypass is recorded on the Trail. 2. Select Approve when your review is complete. 3. Select Merge after every blocking gate passes. Shell Shell Shell One-Time Runner Setup For Repo Owners Repository owners configure runners (the repo evaluators) and gates (the verification checks) once. Everyone else can start with the workflow above. Add the Default Runners 1. Open a terminal at the repository root. 2. Create the default runner configuration: entire runner setup -y 3. Commit the generated runner files to the default branch: git add .entire/runners git commit -m \"Configure trail runners\" 4. Push and merge the runner configuration. The defaul\ntrail-confidence.json\ntrail-drift.json\ntrail-review.json\ntrail-risk.json\ntrail-security.json\ntrail-summary.json\n{\n  \"id\": \"trail-review\",\n  \"display_name\": \"Trail Review\",\n  \"enabled\": true,\n  \"scope\": \"trail\",\n  \"runtime\": {\n    \"kind\": \"prompt_runner\",\n    \"agent\": \"claude\",\n    \"model\": \"sonnet\",\n    \"timeout_ms\": 900000,\n    \"sandbox\": {\n      \"base_template\": \"claude\",\n      \"repo_token\": \"read\",\n      \"auto_stop_minutes\": 20\n    }\n  },\n  \"automation\": {\n    \"kind\": \"trail_prompt\"\n  },\n  \"prompt\": {\n    \"template\": \"You are reviewing the changes on branch \\\"{{branch}}\\\" against \\\"{{base_branch}}\\\". Raise comments only for real bugs, regressions, security issues, or data-loss risks tied to concrete code in the diff. Each finding needs a severity (high, medium, or low).\\n\\nPrevious open findings on","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"72d00ae9-a6e2-49a6-89d9-4cbb2b8e8f98","timestamp":"2026-10-10T22:10:53.042Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"2. Build the Change 3. Review the Trail 4. Approve and Merge One-Time Runner Setup For Repo Owners Add the Default Runners Configure Merge Gates Shell Shell Key Terms First, a few definitions, so you can speak our language. Checkpoint: a checkpoint is basically an autosave, a point at which your work is saved without committing. Finding: an issue with your code that prevents a trail from merging. Gate: a merge requirement. Runner: evaluation metrics that inform the gates. Monitor: a judgment “scorecardˮ monitoring verification status against the runners. Ours run Risk, Confidence, Security, and Slop. If You Donʼt Already Have Entire Setup The steps below assume Entire is installed on your machine and your repository is connected to entire.io with trails turned on. If your team has already done this, start at step 1. 1. Install the CLI brew tap entireio/tap brew trust entireio/tap brew install --cask entire On Linux, run curl -fsSL https://entire.io/install.sh | bash . On Windows, use Scoop: scoop bucket add entire https://github.com/entireio/scoop-bucket.git , then scoop install entire/entire. 2. Confirm the Install entire version Shell Shell 3. Sign In to Entire entire login 4. Enable Entire in Your Repository This installs the Git and agent hooks and creates the .entire/ settings your team shares: entire enable Pass --agent to skip the prompt, for example entire enable --agent claude-code. 5. Connect the Repository to entire.io Open repository setup, select Configure GitHub App, and give Entire access to the repository. A repository owned by a GitHub organization needs an organization admin to install or update the app. 6. Turn On Trails Open the repository in entire.io, select Settings , then turn on: - Runners - Trails under Workflow - Auto build on push. This requires admin permission on the repository. Once the repository appears in entire.io with trails turned on, continue with the steps below. If You Do Already Have Entire Setup 0. Turn on Trails if you havenʼt already (see above) 1. Create a Trail In entire.io 1. Open a repository and select Trails. 2. Select New Trail. Shell Shell Shell 3. Enter a title and, if you like, a description. A good description covers the problem, intended outcome, and any important constraints or acceptance criteria. 4. Choose the status, type, assignees, and priority. 5. Leave the Trail branchless, enter a new branch name, or link an existing branch. 6. Select Create Trail. In the CLI 1. Open a terminal in your Git repository. 2. Run the interactive creation flow: entire trail create 3. To skip the form, create a Trail for the current branch by passing a title: entire trail create --title \"Add account recovery\" Add --checkout to switch to a new branch after Entire creates it from the title. 2. Build the Change 1. Work on the Trailʼs branch with your coding agent. Entire adds the resulting checkpoints and agent sessions to the Trail. 2. Commit your changes when theyʼre ready for evaluation: a. Tell your agent to add and commit b. OR git add . git commit -m \"Add account recovery\" 3. Push the branch: a. ask the agent to push the trail. The agent recognizes the trail connected to the branch with the help of the CLI and it will take care of committing and pushing. b. OR Shell git push 3. Review the Trail 1. Read the description and confirm that the implementation matches the intended outcome. 2. Check the Confidence , Risk , Security , and Drift monitors for anything that needs attention. 3. Select View changes and review the diff. 4. If there are code review findings, open each finding and decide whether to fix, resolve, or dismiss it. 5. Use Sessions and the timeline when you need the agent context behind a decision. After another push, findings tied to older code may become stale. Current findings remain open until they are resolved or dismissed. Since your coding agent interfaces with Entire through the CLI, you can also complete the loop from your agent, for example by telling the agent to review open findings on the trail and address them. 4. Approve and Merge Gate What it Evaluates Options Up to date Whether the Trail branch is current with its base Allow a clean merge while behind Human review Approvals submitted on the Trail Minimum approvals, whether the author counts, and whether pushes reset approvals Agent review Unresolved runner findings on the latest push Minimum severity that counts as unresolved Checks CI checks reported through GitHub Use GitHub-required checks or provide specific check names 1. Review the gates section for anything blocking the merge. Gates show which conditions are satisfied, pending, or blocking the merge. A repository can require an up-to-date branch, human approvals, an agent review without unresolved findings, and passing checks. You can also choose who may bypass a blocking gate. Every bypass is recorded on the Trail. 2. Select Approve when your review is complete. 3. Select Merge after every blocking gate passes. Shell Shell Shell One-Time Runner Setup For Repo Owners Repository owners configure runners (the repo evaluators) and gates (the verification checks) once. Everyone else can start with the workflow above. Add the Default Runners 1. Open a terminal at the repository root. 2. Create the default runner configuration: entire runner setup -y 3. Commit the generated runner files to the default branch: git add .entire/runners git commit -m \"Configure trail runners\" 4. Push and merge the runner configuration. The defaul\ntrail-confidence.json\ntrail-drift.json\ntrail-review.json\ntrail-risk.json\ntrail-security.json\ntrail-summary.json\n{\n  \"id\": \"trail-review\",\n  \"display_name\": \"Trail Review\",\n  \"enabled\": true,\n  \"scope\": \"trail\",\n  \"runtime\": {\n    \"kind\": \"prompt_runner\",\n    \"agent\": \"claude\",\n    \"model\": \"sonnet\",\n    \"timeout_ms\": 900000,\n    \"sandbox\": {\n      \"base_template\": \"claude\",\n      \"repo_token\": \"read\",\n      \"auto_stop_minutes\": 20\n    }\n  },\n  \"automation\": {\n    \"kind\": \"trail_prompt\"\n  },\n  \"prompt\": {\n    \"template\": \"You are reviewing the changes on branch \\\"{{branch}}\\\" against \\\"{{base_branch}}\\\". Raise comments only for real bugs, regressions, security issues, or data-loss risks tied to concrete code in the diff. Each finding needs a severity (high, medium, or low).\\n\\nPrevious open findings on","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"785c0818-aab1-488a-bf5f-e4aeeef14b39","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"53e0d48f-0e86-4176-8390-70cad3fe3f46","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Volumes/PJ/peresjav/hackathons/ehl-final-minuch-2026","sessionId":"7fd52f13-5d0c-4e7f-8ec6-8a947b2499a1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"federated-petting-ripple"}
